<section id="getting-started">
    <h2>Getting started</h2>
    <p>This is the shortest way from an empty page to a page that shows in the menu. Each step points to the part that explains it.</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>CMS &gt; Pages</strong> and click <em>New page</em>. Type the title, pick the parent page and the template, then click <em>Save</em>.</li>
        <li>On the screen of the page, click <em>Edit content</em>.</li>
        <li>Write the content. With the generator: say in the brief what the page should hold, then click <em>Write the draft</em>. By hand: click <em>Or add a section by hand</em> and pick a component. See <a href="#content">The content of a page</a>.</li>
        <li>Look at the preview on the right. <em>Preview the draft</em> opens it in a new tab.</li>
        <li>Click <em>Publish</em>, at the top of the content screen, or <em>Save and publish</em> at the bottom of the settings. The message "The page is online." confirms it. See <a href="#publish-first-page">Publishing your first page</a>.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>CMS &gt; Menus</strong>, then the menu you want (<code>main</code> is the main menu). Under <em>Add an entry</em>, pick <em>Page</em> in <em>This entry points at</em>, select the page and click <em>Add to the menu</em>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Each language of the site is worked on its own: pick it with the language selector before you start. If a button is greyed out or publishing is refused, see <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>The CMS manages the pages of the site, their content and everything around them. It all sits in the <strong>CMS</strong> section of the left-hand menu, where you only see the screens your profile allows.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Pages</strong>: the tree of the site, the content of each page, its publication.</li>
        <li><strong>Page templates</strong>: the models of a series of pages (a recipe, a case study, a news item), with the values each page of the series carries.</li>
        <li><strong>Tags</strong>: words that gather pages, such as "News".</li>
        <li><strong>Folders</strong>: the folders and contents of the shop, which are still managed apart.</li>
        <li><strong>Menus</strong>, <strong>Forms</strong>, <strong>Media</strong>: the navigation, the contact forms and the image library.</li>
        <li><strong>Components</strong> and <strong>Skills</strong>: the blocks pages are made of and the instructions the generator follows.</li>
        <li><strong>Settings</strong>: the mode of the site, maintenance, the error page, the generator.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>On every screen of the CMS, the <em>Help on this screen</em> link, at the top right, opens the part of this documentation that explains it.</p>
    <p>Every page exists in every language of the site. The language selector at the top of the screens picks the language you edit: title, address, content and publication are set language by language.</p>
</section>

<section id="pages">
    <h2>Pages</h2>
    <h3 id="pages-list">Finding a page</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Pages</strong> shows the tree of the site one level at a time. A page that holds others opens and closes on its arrow, without loading the screen again; a branch shows its first 20 pages and a link to the others. The <em>Find a page</em> field looks for a word in the titles of the language edited, and the publication state or the visibility narrow the search.</p>
    <p>The number shown next to a page opens the list of the pages it holds, in the order of the site, 50 per screen. The breadcrumb at the top goes up a level or back to <em>All pages</em>, and <em>Create a page here</em> makes a page right under it. A search typed on that list stays inside the page; removing the <em>Inside "…"</em> chip searches the whole site. On the results of a search, each page named under a title (<em>Under</em> …) opens its list too. The arrows that change the order of pages show on the tree and on the list of the pages of a page, with no search or sort.</p>
    <figure class="figure d-block my-3">
        <img class="figure-img img-fluid rounded border" src="{{ cms_asset('assets/documentation/pages-list.en_US.png') }}" alt="The list of pages: the tree of the site, with the title, the publication state, the address and the actions of each page." width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption class="figure-caption">The list of pages: one row per page, its actions on the right.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Each row shows the state of the page in the language edited: <em>Draft</em>, <em>Scheduled</em>, <em>Published</em>, <em>Modified since publication</em> or <em>Unpublished</em>. <em>Hide</em> takes the page off the site, in every language; <em>Show</em> puts it back.</p>

    <h3 id="page-settings">Creating and setting up a page</h3>
    <p>A click on the title of a page opens its settings, in tabs: <em>General</em>, the <em>Fields</em> of the template when it has some, <em>Search engines and sharing</em>, <em>Image</em> and <em>Documents</em>. A page carries, in each language: a title, an address, a summary, a detailed description and its search engine information (title and description for search engines, social sharing, canonical page, instructions to robots). It also has, for all languages: a parent page, a template, a tag, an image and a publication period (<em>Publish on</em> and <em>Unpublish on</em>, both optional).</p>
    <p>Settings are live as soon as they are saved: only the content goes through a draft and a publication. The <em>Image</em> tab picks the image of the page in the media library, with the same picker as the sections (thumbnails, search, filters), or sends a new one. This image is the one on the cards that present the page elsewhere on the site and in social sharing, unless the <em>Search engines and sharing</em> tab picks another one for sharing.</p>

    <h3 id="page-address">The address of a page</h3>
    <p>The address is made of those of the parent pages and the page's own address: a "Consulting" page filed under "Our services" answers on <code>/our-services/consulting</code>. Leave the <em>Page address</em> field empty and it is taken from the title.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Under the field, the screen shows the full address the page will answer on in the language edited. <em>Regenerate from the title</em> fills the field from the title: once the page is saved, renaming it does not change its address by itself, and the screen warns when the address no longer follows the title.</li>
        <li>When another page, or the same page in another language, already answers on an address, a number is added (<code>cookies-2</code>) and the screen says so. Nothing changes before you save.</li>
        <li>The <em>Fixed address</em> field gives the page a whole address, kept as typed wherever the page sits in the tree, for example <code>about-us.html</code>. It keeps the addresses of a former site. While you type, the screen says whether the address is free. Empty it to go back to the built address.</li>
        <li>Some addresses are reserved for how the site works (<code>admin</code>, <code>api</code>, <code>search</code>...) and refused.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="change-address">Changing the address of a page without breaking links</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>In <strong>CMS &gt; Pages</strong>, pick the language with the selector, then click the title of the page.</li>
        <li>In the <em>General</em> tab, type the new address in <em>Page address</em>, or click <em>Regenerate from the title</em>. To move the page, change the <em>Parent page</em> instead.</li>
        <li>Check the full address shown under the field, then click <em>Save</em>. The address changes at once, without publishing the page again.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The old address stays active and sends visitors to the new one with a permanent redirect (301): links already published and search engines follow. The pages filed under the moved page change address with it and keep their old addresses as redirects. Giving a page a <em>Fixed address</em> also keeps its former address as a redirect. A move in the tree applies to every language of the page, a change of address only to the language edited.</p>

    <h3 id="brief-field">Writing a summary or a description from a brief</h3>
    <p>Next to the <em>Summary</em> and the <em>Detailed description</em> of a page, and next to the summary, description and conclusion of a product, a category, a brand, a folder and a content, <em>Write from a brief</em> asks the generator for the text of that field. Say what it should say, and if you wish its tone and its audience: the generator also reads the title and what the field already holds (to rewrite or complete it), and may search the products, categories and pages of the site to cite them or link to them.</p>
    <p>The text comes back formatted (paragraphs, bold, italic, lists, links, headings) and is shown first, with its cost. <em>Replace the text</em> puts it in place of the content of the field, <em>Insert at the cursor</em> adds it where the cursor was, <em>Cancel</em> leaves the field as it was. Nothing is saved before the form is saved, as if you had typed the text. The button takes the right to use the generator; its effort and length are set in <strong>CMS &gt; Settings</strong>, its model in the AI settings of Thelia.</p>

    <h3 id="page-actions">Home page, duplicating, unpublishing</h3>
    <p>These three actions sit at the bottom of the settings screen of a page.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Set as home page</em> makes the page answer on the address <code>/</code>. Its own address then redirects to the home page.</li>
        <li><em>Duplicate</em> creates a hidden copy of the page, with its tag and its content as a draft, to be read again before you publish it.</li>
        <li><em>Unpublish</em> takes the page off the site in the language edited. The draft and the settings are kept: <em>Publish</em> puts the page back online.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="trash">Bin</h3>
    <p>A page you delete goes to the <strong>Bin</strong> (<strong>CMS &gt; Pages &gt; Bin</strong>) with the pages filed under it. It stays there 30 days, a period set in the settings, and <em>Restore</em> puts it back in the tree until then. A page filed under a page that is itself in the bin comes back with it. After that it is deleted for good, content and addresses included.</p>

    <h3 id="documents">Attaching documents to a page</h3>
    <p>The <em>Documents</em> tab of a saved page attaches files to download: a data sheet, a catalogue, a form. You can choose several at once; PDF, Word, Excel and PowerPoint files, OpenDocument text, ZIP files and plain text are accepted, up to the size the server allows, shown on the tab. A refused file is reported with its reason, without stopping the others from being added.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The file is the same in every language; the <em>title</em> and the <em>description</em> are written in the language being edited. Without a title, the file is shown under its original name.</li>
        <li>The order is set with the arrows of each document; it is the order in which the theme lists them.</li>
        <li>Each action of the tab (adding, text, order, deleting) is saved at once, without <em>Save</em>, and the documents are online right away, without publishing the page again. Deleting a document also deletes its file: a link written to it in a page stops working.</li>
        <li>A duplicated page gets its own copy of each file. The files of a page deleted for good, when the <strong>Bin</strong> is emptied, are deleted with it.</li>
        <li>Adding or changing a document takes the right to change pages, deleting one the right to delete them.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="content">
    <h2>The content of a page</h2>
    <p>The content is edited on its own screen: <em>Edit content</em>, from the page. It is made of <strong>sections</strong> laid one under the other, each one a component (a banner, a text, a list of products, a form...). On the left, the brief and the list of sections; on the right, the preview shows the draft the way the site will display it.</p>
    <figure class="figure d-block my-3">
        <img class="figure-img img-fluid rounded border" src="{{ cms_asset('assets/documentation/content-screen.en_US.png') }}" alt="The content screen: the list of sections on the left, the preview of the page on the right." width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption class="figure-caption">Sections are set on the left, the draft is read in the preview on the right.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 id="generator">Writing with the generator</h3>
    <p>On an empty page, the brief asks what the page should say, its tone and its audience. <em>Write the draft</em> sends the brief to the generator, which answers with a whole page. To write, it follows the skills of the site, picks among the components offered and may search the products, categories, pages and images of the site. If the settings allow it, it also reads the web pages the brief cites by address. On a page already written, <em>Replace the draft</em> starts the page over, after a confirmation.</p>
    <p>To touch up rather than start over, use <em>Ask for a change</em> then <em>Apply the change</em>: the whole page is reworked, and the sections the change does not concern are kept as they are. <em>Rewrite this section</em>, on a section of the list, reworks that section alone.</p>
    <p>The brief, <em>Ask for a change</em> and <em>Rewrite this section</em> take a <code>.md</code>, <code>.txt</code> or <code>.pdf</code> file of up to 5 MB and 100,000 characters of text: a product sheet, a page of the old site, new prices. Only the text of a PDF is read: not its images, and not a scanned PDF, which is refused, nor a PDF too heavy to read (mostly drawings, plans), whose text is joined as a <code>.txt</code> file instead. A text saved by Windows, in its own encoding or as Unicode, is read as it is. The generator draws from it, never takes orders from it: what to do is written in the text box. A file that gives instructions is refused before anything is written; the whole file is checked, however long. If the AI settings of the shop leave this check out of the guardrails of page writing, a file is refused, and the message says where to put it back. The file is not kept; only its name is.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The <em>History</em> of the screen lists each request, with its result, its duration and its cost.</li>
        <li>The draft a request replaces is kept: <em>Previous drafts</em> lists the last five, and <em>Put it back</em> puts one in place of the current draft, which is kept in turn.</li>
        <li>Using the generator takes a right of its own, because each call is billed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="brief-flow">What happens after <em>Write the draft</em></h3>
    <p>The diagram follows a request, from the click to the draft. Meanwhile, the screen shows each step as it happens. You may close the tab: the request goes to its end and stays in the history.</p>
    {{ include('@TheliaCMSModule/backOffice/default-twig/documentation/_brief_flow.html.twig', {
        lang: 'en_US',
        title: 'The way of a brief, from the click to the draft',
        summary: 'You write the brief; TheliaCMS prepares the request; the AI layer of Thelia checks it and sends it to the provider; the model writes the page and may ask TheliaCMS for tools; TheliaCMS checks the document, sends it back to be fixed when needed, then saves the draft; you read it and publish.',
        lanes: ['You', 'TheliaCMS', 'Thelia AI layer', 'AI provider'],
        steps: [
            ['You write the brief,', 'then click', 'Write the draft'],
            ['Prepares the request:', 'skills, offered sections,', 'site, page and brief'],
            ['Checks the usage, picks', 'the model, reserves', 'the budget'],
            ['The model writes', 'the page as sections'],
            ['Tools: products, pages,', 'images, forms,', 'addresses of the brief'],
            ['Checks the document:', 'catalogue sections,', 'template fields'],
            ['Saves the draft, keeps', 'the former one, logs', 'the cost in the history'],
