    }) }}
    <ol>
        <li><strong>You</strong> write the brief, and its tone and audience if needed. On a page already written, the button reads <em>Replace the draft</em> and asks for a confirmation.</li>
        <li><strong>TheliaCMS prepares the request.</strong> It puts in the skills of the site, the list of the sections the generator may use, the name, language and currency of the site, the title, template and summary of the page, the instructions and fields of the template, then your brief. The web addresses a brief cites are read only when the setting <em>Read the web pages a brief cites</em> is on.</li>
        <li><strong>The AI layer of Thelia</strong> checks that the usage is active, picks the connection and model set in its settings, checks the number of requests and reserves the budget. A spent budget stops the request here, with a message.</li>
        <li><strong>The AI provider</strong> has the model write the page, section by section.</li>
        <li><strong>The tools.</strong> To write accurately, the model may ask TheliaCMS to search products, categories, pages, images of the library or forms, to read the sheet of a component or a skill, to read an address of the brief, or to make a component of the site when the setting <em>Let a brief make a component of the site</em> is on and your profile allows it. The result goes back to the model, 12 turns at most.</li>
        <li><strong>TheliaCMS checks the document.</strong> Each section has to come from the catalogue, each field of the template has to be filled as it should. A refused document goes back to the model with the list of its problems, twice at most; then the request fails and nothing is changed.</li>
        <li><strong>TheliaCMS saves the draft.</strong> The HTML is cleaned, the draft keeps the AI provenance mark when the AI settings of Thelia set it, the former draft goes to <em>Previous drafts</em>, and the history notes the duration, the turns and the cost.</li>
        <li><strong>You</strong> see the preview reloaded and a message that sums up the request, with what the brief asked for and the page could not cover. Nothing is online until you click <em>Publish</em>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="translate">Writing another language from one already written</h3>
    <p>When a page has no content yet in the chosen language but has some in another, the preview offers a button, <em>Write it in this language from the</em> source language <em>version</em> (with a list of languages when there are several). The generator keeps the sections and writes their texts for the new reader. The result arrives as a draft, to be read before you publish. It takes the right to use the generator. The items of the catalogue (products, categories, contents, folders) work the same way.</p>
    <p>For a page, the header is written in the same call: title, summary, description and the two sharing texts. Only what is empty in that language is written, so what you typed there is kept, and a message lists what was written and what was left as it was. If the language has no address yet, it is made from the title; an address that exists is never changed. Later, if the header of the source language changes, the page is flagged <em>To be translated again</em> like it is for a section. <em>Write it again from the</em> source language <em>version</em> only fills the header fields that are empty, like the first translation: a header already filled in this language is not rewritten, yet the flag goes away. Update such a header by hand in the settings of the page.</p>

    <h3 id="by-hand">Editing a section in its form</h3>
    <p>On the row of a section, <em>Edit by hand</em> opens a form that shows its fields: text, rows to add or remove for a list, a picker for an image or a product. Hovering over a section in the preview also shows <em>Edit</em>, which opens the same form, and <em>Delete</em>, which removes it after a confirmation.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The form closes with the cross or the Escape key.</li>
        <li>The source of an image other than the media library and the appearance of the section are folded under their heading.</li>
        <li>Width, spacing and alignment are chosen on buttons that draw them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="by-hand-inline">Editing a text in the preview</h3>
    <p>A double click on a free text in the preview makes it editable where it stands, with the toolbar of the editor. <em>Save</em> or Ctrl+Enter keeps the text, <em>Cancel</em> or Escape puts it back.</p>

    <h3 id="by-hand-image">Putting an image in a section</h3>
    <p>The image of a section is shown with its name and whether it is described. <em>Choose in the library</em> opens the media library as thumbnails, with search, filters, tags and the sending of a new image (see <a href="#add-described-image">Adding a described image</a>). An image of the media library is published with the alternative text of the library; the text of the section is only used for an image kept elsewhere, or until the image is described. Choosing an image in a section takes the right to see media.</p>

    <h3 id="by-hand-image-size">Setting the size and the framing of an image</h3>
    <p>In the form of an image section, <em>Width</em> offers three widths (narrow, as wide as the content, the whole width of the screen) and <em>A size in pixels</em>. With the latter, <em>Width in pixels</em> (160 to 2400) sets the width of the picture and <em>Height in pixels</em> (80 to 2000, no more stretched than 4:1 or 1:4) gives it a frame; the buttons 16:9, 4:3, 3:2, 1:1 and <em>Image ratio</em> fill the height from the width. On a narrow screen the frame shrinks and keeps its proportions. With a height, <em>Fill the frame, crop the rest</em> crops the picture to the frame and <em>Show the whole picture</em> leaves empty bands if the proportions differ.</p>
    <p><em>Crop the image…</em> opens a window with the whole picture and the part kept lit, and what the frame will show. Drag the zone with the mouse or a finger, or click to put its centre there; with the keyboard, the arrows move it by 1 % (10 % with Shift), <kbd>+</kbd> and <kbd>-</kbd> (or Page Up and Page Down) zoom by 10 %, Home puts the centre back and Escape closes the window. The three sliders carry the same numbers. A line tells whether the part kept is sharp at the size shown. <em>Confirm</em> writes the numbers in the form, which are kept when the section is saved; <em>Cancel</em> leaves them as they were. The button is off, with its reason below it, when no image is chosen, when the image is not in the media library of the CMS or when its size cannot be read. For an image kept elsewhere only the centre can be set; the zoom stays at 100 %.</p>
    <p>For the three widths of an image section, <em>Proportions</em> offers <em>Original image</em> (as it is, the default), 16:9, 4:3, 3:2, 1:1 and 3:4: the picture then fills a frame of that shape, whose height follows the width, around the part kept. <em>Crop the image…</em> is there for every width; with <em>Original image</em>, only the centre and the zoom can be set (zoom 100 is the whole picture). With <em>A size in pixels</em>, the proportions are not offered: the frame is the width and the height.</p>
    <p>In a columns section, <em>Proportions of the images</em> applies to all the columns: <em>Band (logos)</em> (the default) shows each picture whole in a strip of the same height; the other choices give each picture the width of its column, in the shape chosen. The part kept is set column by column, with <em>Crop the image…</em> under the picture of each one.</p>
    <p>The pictures of the components of the theme and of the components the module made from a brief have the same settings: under each picture (and in each row of a list), <em>Proportions</em> and <em>Crop the image…</em>. The component keeps its own frame: if its box is shaped differently from the proportions chosen, the theme crops again. The dialog only knows the shape asked for, not that box. For a picture kept elsewhere, cropping is off: the component shows it as it is.</p>
    <p>On publication, the media library serves the part kept, in the proportions of the frame. A page already published keeps the files it was published with: after changing an image of the library, run <code>php Thelia thelia_cms:render-published</code> to render the published pages again. A change asked of the generator, or a section it writes again, keeps the size and the framing the editor set, as long as the image of the section stays the same.</p>

    <h3 id="by-hand-icon">Choosing an icon in a section</h3>
    <p>An icon field shows the name chosen and a <em>Choose an icon</em> button. It opens a grid of previews with a search field, in two groups: the icons of the theme and those of the CMS (the SVG files of the library that have a name). Click an icon to choose it. <em>No icon</em>, when it is offered, empties the field; it is not offered for a required field. If the grid cannot load, the field stays a text field where you type the name: <code>cms:arrow-right</code> for a CMS icon, the bare name for an icon of the theme.</p>
    <p>The name is checked for its shape only: an icon deleted since does not make the page invalid, the section simply draws nothing in its place. The generator knows only the icons that exist: an unknown icon in a page it writes is sent back to it to be corrected, then the field is emptied if it is not.</p>

    <h3 id="by-hand-sections">Adding, duplicating, moving, removing sections</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Add a section</em>, under the last one, offers the components of the site: the one you choose lands at the end of the page with example text to replace. An empty page starts this way, without the generator.</li>
        <li>In <em>Appearance</em>, the room button <em>Precise room</em> shows eight fields that set the room of the section side by side: inside (the background goes with it) and outside (above and below, between the section and its neighbours; left and right, the band is narrower), top, bottom, left and right. On a phone, a side on the left or the right takes 5% of the screen at most. Write a number followed by <code>px</code> (0 to 320) or <code>rem</code> (0 to 20), such as <code>64px</code> or <code>2rem</code>; an empty field leaves the room of the component. The buttons Small, Medium and Large set the room inside, above and below together; choosing one hides the eight fields and leaves them out of the save.</li>
        <li>The <em>Duplicate this section</em> button, next to the arrows of a section, puts a copy right after it in the draft: same component, same settings and same appearance, to be changed afterwards. A section of a component the site has put away is not copied.</li>
        <li>Sections move by drag and drop or with the arrows.</li>
        <li><em>Remove this section</em> deletes it from the draft, after a confirmation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Adding, duplicating, moving or removing a section only changes the draft of the language edited, and takes the right to change pages.</p>

    <h3 id="preview-publish">Previewing and publishing</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Saving never changes what visitors see: you work on a draft.</li>
        <li>The preview shows the <em>whole page</em>, with the header, the footer and the title of the site, or the <em>content only</em>, the sections with nothing around them; the choice is kept from one page to the next.</li>
        <li><em>Preview the draft</em> gives a link valid for 72 hours, to send to someone with no account. A list sets the width of the preview to those the theme provides (phone, tablet, screen).</li>
        <li><em>Publish</em> puts the draft online. An empty page, or one that still holds the example text of a legal page supplied at installation, is refused. The structure of the headings is checked: depending on the settings, a problem is reported or blocks publication.</li>
        <li>Each publication keeps a version of the page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each language has its own content: the French and the English version of a page are two independent drafts, published separately.</p>

    <h3 id="publish-first-page">Publishing your first page</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>In <strong>CMS &gt; Pages</strong>, open the content of the page with the <em>Edit content</em> button on its row.</li>
        <li>Read the draft in the preview. To see it on the site before anyone else, click <em>Preview the draft</em>.</li>
        <li>If the template of the page has required fields, fill them in: on the settings screen of the page, they are in the <em>Fields</em> tab.</li>
        <li>Click <em>Publish</em>, at the top of the content screen. The message "The page is online." appears.</li>
        <li>Check the state shown next to the title: it should read <em>Published</em>. <em>Unpublished</em> means the page is hidden or its withdrawal date has passed; <em>Scheduled</em>, that its publication date has not come yet. Both are set in the <em>General</em> tab of the settings.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The <em>Publish</em> button of the settings screen of the page does the same. <em>Save and publish</em> first saves what is typed in the settings, then publishes the saved content: a required field typed in the <em>Fields</em> tab is taken into account. When the page is hidden or scheduled, the message says so instead of "The page is online." The settings are live as soon as they are saved; only the content has a draft. If publication is refused, the message says why: see <a href="#publication-refused">Publication is refused</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="page-templates">
    <h2>Page templates</h2>
    <p>A page template is the model of a series of pages: recipes, case studies, news. Each page has a template, chosen in its <em>General</em> tab. The templates of the site are in <strong>CMS &gt; Page templates</strong> (also reached from the settings). The <code>default</code> template always exists.</p>
    <h3 id="template-settings">What a template sets</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The display.</strong> The theme of the site may draw the pages of a template its own way. The templates screen shows, for each one, the layout used.</li>
        <li><strong>A name and a description</strong> in each language.</li>
        <li><strong>A brief</strong>, with a tone and an audience, that the generator reads before the one of each page of the template: it says what its pages are about.</li>
        <li><strong>Writing instructions</strong>, in markdown: how all the pages of the template are written (tone, length, expected sections, mandatory mentions). The generator reads them for each page of the template, like a skill reserved for that template, and they win over a brief that says otherwise.</li>
        <li><strong>Fields</strong>: the information each page of the template carries, such as a preparation time or a city. A field is a whole or decimal number, a duration in minutes, a short or long text, a yes or no, a word taken from a list, or a date. It may have a unit, bounds, be written in each language (for texts) or be required: a page is not published while it is empty. The values are filled in the <em>Fields</em> tab of the page settings, and the generator writes them with the content.</li>
        <li><strong>A model page</strong>, the example content new pages of the template start from. It is never published or online, and it is deleted with its template, never put in the bin.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Create from the template</em>, in the <em>Start a page from a template</em> box of the pages list, copies the model page (content in every language and field values) under the title you type.</p>
    <h3 id="template-lists">Listing the pages of a template</h3>
    <p>The <em>Pages of a type</em> section, added in the content of a page, shows the online pages of a template: the latest news, all the case studies. It is set with a title, an order (most recent, by title, by position in the tree, by a field) and a number of pages. The list is read at every visit: a page published tomorrow shows up in it without publishing again the page that holds it.</p>
    <p>A template used by a page, even one in the bin, cannot be deleted.</p>
</section>

<section id="tags">
    <h2>Tags</h2>
    <p>A page carries one tag or none, chosen in its <em>General</em> tab. Tags are created, renamed and deleted in <strong>CMS &gt; Tags</strong>. The theme uses them to gather pages, for example "the other news". Deleting a tag removes it from the pages that carried it; the screen says how many.</p>
    <h3 id="tag-colors">The color of a tag</h3>
    <p>A tag can have a badge in the color of your choice. Tick <em>Give the tag a color</em>, then pick the <em>Background</em> with the color picker.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>By default, the text of the badge is black or white, whichever reads best on the background.</li>
        <li>To choose the color of the text yourself, untick <em>Text in black or white, whichever reads best on the background</em> and set <em>Text</em>.</li>
        <li>The badge shows in the back office; a theme may show it on the site too.</li>
        <li>Changing the color of a tag changes the badge on every page that carries it.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="catalogue">
    <h2>The content of products, categories and contents</h2>
    <p>A product, a category, a content and a folder of the shop can also receive sections, language by language. Their sheet in the back office has a <strong>CMS content</strong> tab, which says where their content stands in each language and opens its screen with <em>Write the content</em> or <em>Edit the content</em>. That screen works like the one of a page: generator, editing by hand, duplicating a section, writing from another language, preview, publication. The generator knows which sheet the content shows on and what it already shows, to complete the sheet rather than repeat its name or its price.</p>
    <p>The preview shows the draft two ways: in the <em>whole page</em> of the sheet, in its place, or as <em>content only</em>, without the header and footer of the site. <em>Take offline</em>, in the tab, puts the content of a language offline; its draft is kept.</p>
</section>

<section id="components">
    <h2>Components</h2>
    <p>The sections of a page come from a catalogue of components, of three families:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Theme components</strong>: the blocks the theme of the site makes available to pages (banners, cards, product lists, videos...). They are displayed with the layout of the site.</li>
        <li><strong>Free text</strong>: a block of formatted text (headings, paragraphs, lists, links).</li>
        <li><strong>Live content</strong>: a block computed at every visit, such as a form of the site or the list of pages of a template.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="components-screen">The Components screen</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Components</strong> lists the catalogue: every component, where it comes from (the module, the theme, the site) and how many pages use it, drafts included. That number is a button: it opens, on the right of the screen, the list of those pages (title, state, a link to each). Tags, page types and images show their pages the same way. Switching a component off takes it out of what the generator may offer. Nothing else changes: the pages using it keep showing it and its sections stay editable by hand. Free text cannot be switched off. A button opens the toolkit of the theme, which shows every component offered, when the theme has one.</p>
    <h3 id="make-component">Having a component made</h3>
    <p><em>New component</em> describes to the generator what the component should show, what an editor fills in and how it should look. It proposes a version, which you keep, have changed or ask for again. A component kept joins the catalogue as "made on this site". Its texts are written by the pages using it, in every language. Making a component takes the settings right and the custom code right; asking the generator for one, or for a change, takes the generator right too. Keeping, copying and deleting a component do not.</p>
    <h3 id="site-components">Copying, keeping or deleting a component of the site</h3>
    <p>In <strong>CMS &gt; Components</strong>, the <em>Edit</em> button, on a component made on this site, opens its screen, with its preview, its properties and its history.</p>
    <figure class="figure d-block my-3">
        <img class="figure-img img-fluid rounded border" src="{{ cms_asset('assets/documentation/site-component.en_US.png') }}" alt="The screen of a component of the site: its preview and the form to copy it into a new component." width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption class="figure-caption">The screen of a component of the site: the preview, and the copy form below it.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Copy</strong>: <em>Copy into a new component</em> asks for a <em>Code</em> (in PascalCase, for example <code>PriceTableDark</code>, which cannot change afterwards) and a <em>Label</em>, then <em>Copy</em> duplicates the template, the styles and the properties without calling the generator. The copy is a prototype: change it with <em>Ask a change</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep</strong>: a component in prototype is shown on the pages that hold it, but offered to nobody else. <em>Keep for the whole site</em>, on its screen or in the list, offers it to the generator and on the content screen of every page.</li>
        <li><strong>Delete</strong>: <em>Delete the component</em> is final, after a confirmation. It is only possible when no page holds the component, drafts and languages included: otherwise the screen gives the number of pages and the component must first be taken out of each.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Copying, keeping and deleting take the same rights as <em>New component</em>.</p>
    <h3 id="prototype-from-page">Prototyping a component from a page</h3>
    <p>On the content screen of a page, <em>Add a section</em> offers, under the existing components, <em>A new component</em>. Describe it in <em>Prototype a component</em> then click <em>Prototype it</em>: it takes up to a minute. The preview then shows the component in place of the draft.</p>
    <p>The request may come with a <code>.md</code>, <code>.txt</code> or <code>.pdf</code> file, on the same terms as the brief: the plans and their prices, the steps of a timeline. The component draws its sample texts from it; what it shows is written in the text box. A file that gives instructions is refused before anything is written. <em>Ask a change</em> takes a file too.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Keep it and add it to the page</em> adds it at the end of the draft. It stays a prototype: <strong>CMS &gt; Components</strong> then keeps it for the whole site.</li>
        <li><em>Ask a change</em> then <em>Change it</em> proposes a new version.</li>
        <li><em>Discard</em> keeps nothing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This takes the right to use the generator and those of <em>New component</em>. It exists on pages only, not on the items of the catalogue.</p>
</section>

<section id="skills">
    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <p>A skill is an instruction the generator reads before it writes: how a page is structured here, how text is written, what accessibility and search engines ask, how to present the catalogue, how to translate a page, how to stay within the design system of the theme, how a component moves without script. The module ships eight (page structure, copywriting, accessibility, search engines, catalogue, translation, design system, motion); the theme may add more or replace them.</p>
    <p>Under <strong>CMS &gt; Skills</strong>, each skill can be rewritten, switched off and moved: the arrows of the list move it up or down one slot, and the generator reads the skills in that order. A skill <em>always read</em> is given to the generator with every request; a skill read <em>on request</em> is read only when the page calls for it, from its description. A rewritten skill keeps your text when the module is updated, and a button puts the shipped text back.</p>
    <p>An instruction you want followed on every page (a tone, a word to avoid, a mandatory mention) belongs in a skill. One that concerns a series of pages goes in the writing instructions of its template, and one for a single page in its own brief.</p>
</section>

<section id="menus-forms-media">
    <h2>Menus, forms, media</h2>
    <h3 id="menus">Menus</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Menus</strong> holds at least the main menu (<code>main</code>) and the footer one (<code>footer</code>).</p>
    <ul>
        <li>An entry points at a page, a content, a folder, a category or a product of the catalogue, a web address, or at nothing (it is then a group heading). Its <em>Label</em> is optional: left empty, the title of the target is shown.</li>
        <li>An entry pointing at a category or a product follows its address in each language: renaming it or changing its address does not leave the menu behind.</li>
        <li>In the form, categories are shown as their tree and products grouped by their default category.</li>
        <li>The tree goes three levels deep; entries are moved with the buttons or by dragging them.</li>
        <li>An entry whose target is deleted, offline (a hidden category or product included) or unpublished in the language read disappears from the menu in that language, and the screen says why.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="menus-by-language">A menu of its own for a language</h3>
    <p>Each menu has a shared version, which every language reads. The screen of a menu says, for the language picked with the selector, whether it <em>follows the shared menu</em> or <em>has a menu of its own</em>. What you change then applies to every language that follows the shared menu, or to that language alone.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Give this language a menu of its own</em> copies the shared menu for that language. Later changes no longer touch the other languages.</li>
        <li><em>Realign on...</em> replaces the entries of the menu by a copy of another menu of the same code, picked in the list. The confirmation screen gives the number of entries replaced. Nothing is merged and it cannot be undone.</li>
        <li><em>Make the shared menu a copy of this one</em> does the opposite: the shared menu takes the entries of the menu of this language.</li>
        <li><em>Go back to the shared menu</em> deletes the own menu of the language, after a confirmation, and the language reads the shared menu again. An own menu you empty stays empty in its language.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Realigning and going back to the shared menu take the rights to change and to delete menus. The code of a menu is changed on its shared version, for every language at once; deleting the shared menu also deletes the menus of its languages.</p>
    <h3 id="forms">Forms</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Forms</strong> creates forms, placed in a page with the form section. A field is a line of text, an email address, several lines, a drop-down list, a tick box, one choice among several, a phone number, a date or an agreement to be contacted. Recipients are set here. Robots are kept out without a captcha. <em>Answers</em> are read, searched by email address, narrowed to the days they arrived and to the value given to a question with choices, exported (CSV, split by a semicolon when the back office is in French, as Excel expects; JSON) and deleted; each one is <em>new</em>, <em>handled</em> or <em>archived</em>, changed one by one or for a selection, and the list shows the current answers by default, the archived ones a tab away; the <em>Answer summary</em> counts them by day, week or month, by status and by value of the questions with choices, for the same filters; the mail to the recipients leads to the answer; they go on their own after the retention period of the form (365 days by default). Reading and deleting the answers, then exporting them, are two rights of their own, apart from building the forms: they are given per profile under <strong>Configuration &gt; Administrators</strong>.</p>
    <h3 id="media">Media</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Media</strong> keeps the images of the pages (JPEG, PNG, WebP, SVG) and MP4 videos. An image is refused above 10 MB or 25 million pixels, in the library, in a page and in a section; a video is taken up to 50 MB, only from the library (a page and a section take images only), and its type is read in the file, not in its name. The alternative text is required in every language, except for an image marked decorative: it is what screen readers read and what shows when the image does not load. A video has no alternative text and is never decorative: it is not asked to be described and never counts among the images to describe. Each image shows its dimensions, its weight and the pages using it; an image in use cannot be deleted. On publication, images are served in several sizes and in WebP.</p>
    <h3 id="add-described-image">Adding a described image</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>CMS &gt; Media</strong>. Drop the files on the <em>Drop images or videos here, or choose them</em> frame, or click <em>Add files</em>. Several files at once are fine; a refused file is named with its reason, without stopping the others.</li>
        <li>When sending is done, click <em>Describe the N images added</em>.</li>
        <li>For each image, type in <em>Alternative text</em> what it shows, for someone who cannot see it, then press Enter or click <em>Save and next</em>. An image that only decorates is marked with <em>It is decorative</em>; <em>Skip</em> leaves it for later.</li>
        <li>The description is written for the language picked with the selector: repeat it in each language of the site.</li>
        <li>Place the image: in the <em>Image</em> tab of a page, or in the form of a section with <em>Choose in the library</em>. The window that opens shows thumbnails; search, filter, then click the image. It tells whether each image is <em>Described</em>, <em>To describe</em> or <em>Decorative</em>.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="figure d-block my-3">
        <img class="figure-img img-fluid rounded border" src="{{ cms_asset('assets/documentation/media-picker.en_US.png') }}" alt="The window to choose an image: thumbnails, a search field and filters." width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption class="figure-caption">The window to choose an image: the state of each image reads on its thumbnail.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3 id="prepare-svg">Preparing an SVG</h3>
    <p>An SVG file does not go straight into the library: it first goes through the <em>Prepare the SVG</em> screen, one file at a time. The JPEG, PNG and WebP images sent with it are added at once, as usual. When several SVG files are sent together, the top of the screen says where you are (<em>File 2 of 3</em>).</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Cleaning</strong>, required. Whatever could run or load content from elsewhere is removed: scripts, event handlers, links to other files or sites, embedded HTML content, animations. Comments and the data of the software that made the file leave too. The list says what was removed or added (a missing <code>viewBox</code>, for instance).</li>
        <li><strong>Optimization</strong>, also required and lossless: the file gets lighter without any change to how it looks. The title and description written in the file (<code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;desc&gt;</code>) leave it: the alternative text of the image is what describes the drawing. It is the only method offered for now.</li>
        <li><strong>Colors</strong>, optional. A preset can make the colors of the drawing follow the color of the text around it: <em>Keep the colors</em> (the default), <em>Monochrome</em> (every color) or <em>Main color follows the text</em>. With the last one, the screen shows the colors of the drawing as a grid of swatches, the most used first (the one of the most elements; hover a swatch for its code): click another one to make it the main color. The other colors then stay as they are (a green check, for instance) or follow the text, lighter. When a white may follow the text, the screen asks whether it does or stays white: a white drawn over a shape that follows the text would disappear with it. A gradient becomes one plain color, and the screen says so. Below the previews, the result is shown on a light ground, a dark ground and next to a colored text. A color that follows the text only does so where the drawing is placed in the page as an icon: shown as an image, it is drawn in black.</li>
        <li><strong>Usage</strong>, for a file added to the library: <em>Image</em> (the default) or <em>Icon</em>. See <a href="#icons">Icons</a>. The choice is not offered for a file that replaces another one, nor for a file sent from a page or a section.</li>
        <li><em>Add to the media library</em> saves the result; the original is not kept. <em>Do not add this file</em> leaves it out. The images added are then described like the others.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The preview shows the original and the result side by side, on a checkerboard, a light or a dark ground, fitted to the frame or enlarged 1, 4 or 10 times; both previews scroll together; below each, its weight, and below the result the saving.</p>
    <p>A file that cannot be read safely is refused with its reason: declared entities, invalid XML, an encoding other than UTF-8, a file that is not an SVG, more than 5 MB or 50,000 elements, or a drawing without dimensions. An SVG is served as it is, without sizes or WebP, and whole: a crop set in a section does not apply to it. To replace the one of an image, use <em>Replace the file</em> on its sheet: the new file goes through the same screen.</p>
    <p>From a page or a section, <em>Send an image</em> in the picker accepts an SVG too: the same screen opens over the editor, and <em>Add and choose</em> adds it to the library and puts it in the field.</p>
    <h3 id="icons">Icons</h3>
    <p>An icon is an SVG of the library that has a name. It is drawn in the color of the text around it, and chosen by its name in the sections that ask for an icon (see <a href="#by-hand-icon">Choosing an icon in a section</a>).</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Drop an SVG on <strong>CMS &gt; Media</strong>: the <em>Prepare the SVG</em> screen opens.</li>
        <li>Under <em>Usage</em>, choose <em>Icon</em>.</li>
        <li>Check the <em>Icon name</em>, proposed from the file name, then click <em>Add to the media library</em>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The name is made of lowercase letters and digits, separated by single hyphens, 64 characters at most (<code>arrow-right</code>). It is used with <code>cms:</code> in front: <code>cms:arrow-right</code>. A name already taken is refused, with the image that holds it. When you send several SVG files, tick <em>Apply these settings to the following file</em> (or <em>to the N following files</em>) so that each becomes an icon, named after its own file, if it can be one.</p>
    <p>A page keeps of a drawing only simple shapes: lines, curves, circles, rectangles and polygons, with their colors. <em>Icon</em> is therefore greyed out when the file holds anything else, and the screen says what: a gradient or a pattern, some text, a mask, a clipping, a filter, style, classes, an identifier, an embedded image. The file can still be added as an image. A multi-color logo with gradients stays an image.</p>
    <p>For an icon to take the color of the text, choose in the <em>Colors</em> step a preset that makes it follow the text. Otherwise it keeps its colors.</p>
    <p>The sheet of the image shows the name, and how many pages, items and components of the site cite the icon (a page in the trash does not count). A component cites it when its template writes <code>cms_icon('cms:arrow-right')</code>.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Rename the icon</em> and <em>Remove the icon name</em> (the image stays in the library, no longer an icon) are possible only when no page, no item and no component of the site cites the icon: their content would keep the old name, and the icon would disappear from it.</li>
        <li><em>Replace the file</em> keeps the name, provided the new file is an SVG that can be an icon; otherwise the old one stays and the screen says why. A JPEG, PNG or WebP image is refused.</li>
        <li><em>Delete this image</em> is greyed out while a page, even a draft, an item or a component of the site cites the icon, as for an image in use. Once none cites it, deleting it removes the name too.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An icon has no alternative text of its own: its sheet does not ask for one, and it is never <em>To describe</em>. The text depends on where it is placed, and the component gives it: next to a text, the icon is hidden from screen readers; alone in a link or a button, that link or button carries the name (<em>Previous page</em>, for instance).</p>
    <p>A page already published keeps the drawing it was published with. After replacing an icon, the pages that cite it change only at their next publication; nothing is republished by itself. To bring what is online up to date without publishing anything new, run <code>php Thelia thelia_cms:render-published --all</code>.</p>
    <h3 id="media-list">Finding and sorting images</h3>
    <p>The list shows 48 images a page. It narrows to the images still to describe in the language edited, the unused ones, the decorative ones or one tag, and is ordered newest or oldest first, by title or by weight. The search reads the title, the alternative text, the caption and the tags. <em>Describe them one by one</em> shows the images of the list still to describe, one at a time.</p>
    <p>Images ticked on the list take or lose a tag, are marked decorative or not, or are deleted when nothing uses them; those still used are kept and named.</p>
    <h3 id="media-sheet">The sheet of an image</h3>
    <p>A video has a sheet with its title, caption and tags, the file played in place, and no description or crop; a replacing file must be an MP4. The picker of images in a page or a section does not list videos.</p>
    <p>The sheet of an image says, for each language, whether it is described, to describe or decorative. Its tags are suggested from those of the library, and a replacing file is shown before it is saved. <em>Delete this image</em> is greyed out while a page or an item uses the image: see <a href="#image-in-use">An image cannot be deleted</a>. For an icon, it shows its name and how many pages cite it: see <a href="#icons">Icons</a>.</p>
    <h3 id="media-catalogue">The images of the catalogue</h3>
    <p>The images of the catalogue (products, categories, brands, folders, contents) are not in the library on their own: the command <code>php Thelia thelia_cms:media:import-catalogue</code> copies them in, with their titles, alternative texts and tags. Run again, it copies only the new ones. A copy does not follow the catalogue: an image replaced there is copied again.</p>
</section>

<section id="settings">
    <h2>Settings</h2>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Settings</strong> gathers the settings of the site:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>What this site is</strong>: a shop, or a showcase site. In showcase mode, the cart and the checkout are closed and the CMS moves to the top of the menu. Saving showcase mode also creates an <em>Editor</em> profile (pages, menus, media, forms, without the shop or these settings), to assign in <strong>Configuration &gt; Administrators</strong>; its rights can then be changed freely. Going back to shop mode reopens the cart and the checkout in one save.</li>
        <li><strong>Site search</strong>: in showcase mode, the addresses <code>/recherche</code> and <code>/search</code> show a search page. It searches the online pages in the language of the visitor, ten results at a time, and leaves out the pages set to <em>Do not index (noindex)</em>. In shop mode, the search of the theme keeps these addresses.</li>
        <li><strong>Page shown when an address does not exist</strong>: a page of the CMS, served with the 404 code.</li>
        <li><strong>Days spent in the bin before deletion</strong>: 30 by default, 0 to never empty the bin automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Seconds a page is kept by a shared cache</strong>: 0, the default, switches this cache off. To set with whoever looks after the hosting.</li>
        <li><strong>Close the site for maintenance</strong>, with the <strong>page shown during maintenance</strong> and the <strong>addresses that keep seeing the site</strong> (IP addresses). The site then answers that it is coming back soon, which protects its search ranking. The back office and signed-in administrators are still served.</li>
        <li><strong>Generator</strong>: the <em>thinking effort</em>, the <em>maximum length of the answer</em>, the permission to <em>read the web pages cited in the brief</em> (off until you switch it on: the shop server fetches the page and sends its text to the AI provider) and <em>Let a brief make a component of the site</em> (off too: the generator may then make a component, at most two per page, when none of the catalogue fits, and it stays a prototype). A higher effort gives better pages, slower and costlier; only Anthropic models read it, another connection ignores it. The texts written from a brief next to a field have their own effort and their own length. The connection, the model and the budget are set in the AI settings of Thelia, not here.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Page templates, components and skills are set from their own screens, with the same right.</p>
</section>

<section id="permissions">
    <h2>Who can do what</h2>
    <p>Rights are given by profile, in <strong>Configuration &gt; Administrators</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>pages: the tree, the content, publication, sections added, duplicated or moved by hand, tags, the content of the shop sheets, and this documentation;</li>
        <li>the generator: asking the generator to write or change, a right of its own because each call is billed;</li>
        <li>menus; forms and their answers; media;</li>
        <li>settings: site mode, maintenance, error page, generator, page templates, components, skills;</li>
        <li>custom code: embedded frames (<code>iframe</code>) in published content, and the making of components.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A screen or a button your profile does not allow is not offered.</p>
</section>

<section id="troubleshooting">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <h3 id="greyed-button">A button is greyed out or missing</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Publish</em> is greyed out: the page has no content yet. Write it or add a section. The button is missing for the model page of a template, which is never published, and for a profile without the right to change pages.</li>
        <li><em>Write the draft</em>, <em>Replace the draft</em> or <em>Write it in this language from...</em> is greyed out: the screen says "Your profile may read this screen and publish, not ask the generator to write". Ask whoever manages profiles for the right on the generator.</li>
        <li>The <em>Documents</em> tab is greyed out: the page is not saved yet. Save it first.</li>
        <li><em>Delete this image</em> is greyed out: see below.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="publication-refused">Publication is refused</h3>
    <p>The screen says why. The possible cases:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>"This page has no content yet": the draft is empty, or shows nothing. Write it or add a section.</li>
        <li>"This page still holds the sample text it was created with": replace the example texts with your own.</li>
        <li>"This page is the model of its type": a model is never published. Create a new page from the template.</li>
        <li>"Fill in the fields this type requires before publishing" followed by the field names: fill them in the <em>Fields</em> tab of the page settings.</li>
        <li>"Fix the heading structure first": the messages above list the problems, for example a skipped heading level or several level 1 headings. Fix the headings of the sections, then publish again. Depending on how the site is configured, this check blocks publication or lets the page through with a warning.</li>
        <li>"Its content is no longer valid": open the page in the editor, the messages point to the section to fix.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="address-suffix">The address of a page ends with "-2"</h3>
    <p>Another page, or the same page in another language, already answers on that address. The screen announces it before you save, and nothing changes until you do. Change the <em>Page address</em> to another one, or change the address of the other page.</p>
    <h3 id="image-in-use">An image cannot be deleted</h3>
    <p>An image used by a page or an item cannot be deleted. On its sheet, <em>Delete this image</em> is greyed out and the list of pages using it is shown. Take the image out of each (<em>Remove</em> in its <em>Image</em> tab, or in the form of the section), then delete it. In a bulk deletion, images still in use are kept and named.</p>
    <h3 id="generator-unavailable">The generator does not answer</h3>
    <p>The screen gives the reason. Editing by hand and publishing still work. The cases, with what to do:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The AI budget of the site is spent: the generator works again next month, or as soon as the budget is raised in the AI settings of Thelia.</li>
        <li>The generator is turned off, or no AI connection and model is set up for it, or the key of the connection cannot be read: to fix in the AI settings of Thelia.</li>
        <li>The AI provider does not answer, or too many requests were sent: wait a few minutes and try again.</li>
        <li>The answer was cut at the length limit: ask for a shorter page, or raise the limit in <strong>CMS &gt; Settings</strong>.</li>
        <li>The model declined to write: reword the brief.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If it lasts, tell whoever looks after the site.</p>
</section>

<section id="glossary">
    <h2>Glossary</h2>
    <dl>
        <dt>Page</dt>
        <dd>An address of the site, with its title, its settings and its content. It exists in every language.</dd>
        <dt>Template</dt>
        <dd>The model of a series of pages (recipes, news): layout, writing instructions, fields and model page.</dd>
        <dt>Section</dt>
        <dd>A block of the content of a page, laid under the previous one. Each section is a component.</dd>
        <dt>Component</dt>
        <dd>The kind of a section (a banner, a text, a list of products). The theme and the site provide them.</dd>
        <dt>Skill</dt>
        <dd>An instruction the generator reads before it writes, rewritten and switched off from <strong>CMS &gt; Skills</strong>.</dd>
        <dt>Draft</dt>
        <dd>The content being written. Visitors do not see it before it is published.</dd>
        <dt>Tag</dt>
        <dd>A word, with an optional color, that gathers pages.</dd>
        <dt>Generator</dt>
        <dd>The automatic writing by AI: it writes pages, sections, texts and components from a brief.</dd>
    </dl>
</section>
